WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.340
The Guardian Legend.

00:00:01.720 --> 00:00:12.640
Long ago, in a distant galaxy, an alien race sent a huge world, Naju, hurtling towards Earth, loaded with a cargo of mysterious lifeforms.

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:27.900
NOSTALGIA

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:34.940
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:37.040
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:38.520
And I'm Joe.

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:43.800
Joe, the back of the box starts with long ago in a distant galaxy.

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:46.160
That's kind of Star Wars, right?

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:47.860
Like... Doesn't ring any bells.

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:49.280
Doesn't ring any bells at all.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:52.400
But yeah, I mean, I guess they're just, you know, they're going with the tropes.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:57.220
You know, that's a good way to get your space opera fans.

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:04.220
The one example of space opera with galaxies long ago.

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:05.460
Not anymore.

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:10.880
And also, you know, the Guardian Legend, right?

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:15.780
I don't think immediately of like, a shmup, that's for sure.

00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:17.260
I definitely don't think I'm about to play a shmup.

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:19.320
But I also wouldn't expect an adventure game.

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:21.240
The Guardian Legend, to me, sounds like an RPG.

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:23.580
Sean, what kind of game did you think we were going to play?

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:30.240
Yeah, I didn't have any real prediction for what the genre was going to be.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:31.880
But setting, I was way off.

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:38.460
When I heard Guardian Legend, I thought this was going to be sort of like a sword and sorcery sort of thing and not so much a sci-fi thing.

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:43.140
As I said, it does sort of like, give me the vibe of like, that's why I said space opera.

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:50.040
I mean, partially because of Star Wars, but also like, gives me that vibe of like a, like a sword and sorcery thing that was set in a futuristic setting.

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:51.820
So I get it.

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:53.340
Maybe it's just the title that does that for me.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:58.120
The title just confuses me, like, you know, just syntactically, if that's a word.

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:04.760
I think we were talking about this in the sort of, you know, rump of the last episode.

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:11.020
I don't really get what a better, I think like the Legend of the Guardian would make more sense.

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:12.120
But that's just me.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:13.980
Legend of the Guardian sounds good.

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:16.800
But I would argue that the Guardian Legend is just fine.

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:26.060
Because, you know, that would be like, that's more of like the title, you know, the Guardian Legend is what people refer to that legend as.

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:35.700
Any biters? I would say, I think it's kind of, yeah, it's like, it adds to a little bit of a mystery to it.

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:40.840
It's not, it's not quite in the way that like the, the layman vernacular would say it.

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:42.160
Am I a layman to you?

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:44.200
Yes. Oh, okay.

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:53.120
Well, we've talked a lot about the title, and we've talked a lot about what this game could or maybe sounds like it is based on just the title alone.

00:02:53.120 --> 00:03:07.340
But it was kind of surprising to find out that this is an action adventure shmup game, where you control a humanoid robot who can also transform into a spaceship.

00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:11.440
And is obviously known as the Guardian, that's her name.

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:20.940
And in a quest to destroy a large alien infested world, which I think we refer to in the back of the box as Najoo, which could be Naboo.

00:03:21.220 --> 00:03:22.540
Again, just more Star Wars stuff.

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:35.360
However, since this game is 89 and Naboo was 2001 or something, I think, you know, George Lucas probably watched a lot of the Guardian Legend as he was preparing for his prequel films.

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:36.980
Oh yeah, I think I saw that in one of the behind the scenes.

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:38.540
He watched it. The behind the scenes, yeah.

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:40.480
Yeah, he watched it, he didn't play it.

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:42.920
His big brother was playing it, and he had to watch.

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:44.360
Right, right, exactly.

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:46.840
Everybody remembers Steve Lucas.

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:48.300
45, 50 year olds.

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:56.080
So, action shmup, adventure shmup, action adventure shmup, whatever you want to call it.

00:03:56.300 --> 00:04:03.200
You know, have we seen something like this before on the NES, where like two genres get merged into one game?

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:10.580
Yeah. The closest thing we saw was like Blaster Master was kind of like this, right?

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:12.960
Where you get in your vehicle, you get your vehicle combat.

00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:20.700
I guess it's not really necessarily like a different, it doesn't change from like a scrolling shmup into like something else,

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:23.720
but it definitely, it changed the context of each battle.

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:26.200
Yeah, definitely different modes of play.

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:37.560
Anyway, I think this is one of the most, like there's a bigger disparity between the genres that we've seen in prior implementations of this.

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:44.180
Yeah, I don't think we've had a shmup with, maybe we have.

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:46.000
I feel like we had to have, right?

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:51.340
What? A shmup with another kind of game tacked on?

00:04:52.400 --> 00:05:02.140
No, I feel like every shmup we've played so far on the system has been a shmup with maybe some sort of like, you know, experience system attached to it.

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:09.040
You know, I think we've had a couple of games that I wouldn't primarily consider shmups that suddenly had like shmup elements, like the reverse.

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:18.460
Like what was, I think like Golgol 13 had like the helicopter shmup, which is like a little mini game that you do every couple of levels.

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:23.540
And I think there was like one other like that where it's like, oh, now you're on the shmup part and now you're back in your regular part.

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:24.780
Like the shmup was like an aside.

00:05:24.980 --> 00:05:26.800
Yeah. Whereas this, it almost feels like 50-50.

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:34.920
Yeah, we've definitely played games that have like genre combinations, but they're a lot more like half-baked, I'd say.

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:44.640
Yeah. And so the shmup side is very familiar to developer of this game Compile because they're known for making some of the best shmups of all time.

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:55.480
And so I think, you know, there's the shmup part of this game, and we'll break these all down later, but there's the shmup part of this game that is very much like how we've been playing these and how we experienced them.

00:05:55.480 --> 00:06:06.320
But the action-adventure side chose more of a Legend of Zelda and Metroid-style approach where it is that, you know, top-down dungeon interface from Legend of Zelda.

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:16.160
But I also say Metroid because you can choose to skip objects, to wait for later to collect certain things.

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:19.080
You can go back. You can skip certain areas.

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:38.180
So it was interesting to see a game where the, you know, like, it's 50-50 between the shmup and the Zelda part, but the Zelda part is definitely the more consequential part of it because that really depends on what you do in terms of power-ups and upgrades and exploration.

00:06:38.360 --> 00:06:46.320
And the shmup side is more like the minigame explanation of how you travel between the different areas on the planet.

00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:59.480
Yeah, I'd say the time you spend in this Zelda-like sort of setting is much higher than the amount of time you're playing the shmup.

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:07.540
Yeah, but I'll say that, you know, for a group of, well, I can't speak for you guys, obviously, but none of us really love shmups, am I right, normally?

00:07:08.380 --> 00:07:09.380
I'm a pretty big fan.

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:10.980
Okay, maybe I'm projecting.

00:07:13.480 --> 00:07:15.140
Okay, so we got, like, the Goldilocks thing.

00:07:15.140 --> 00:07:24.280
I hate shmups, but I was actually found myself, like, looking forward to the shmup sections, and I think that they found, like, a perfect balance for it.

00:07:24.340 --> 00:07:27.260
Like, it was really, it felt like I was collecting shmup levels.

00:07:27.560 --> 00:07:29.680
Like, it felt like I was, like, that's my goal.

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:33.220
Like, I had to get to the next shmup part, but getting there was really fun and finding it.

00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:38.340
And I found it, and I'm like, oh, here we go, another shmup, which is, like, not what I've ever wanted before.

00:07:38.460 --> 00:07:39.960
The bite-sized shmup, which is the perfect size shmup.

00:07:40.460 --> 00:07:41.260
Yeah, exactly.

00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:45.880
It's like another little thing, like, oh, if I get this, it just gives you a really good sense of progress.

00:07:46.580 --> 00:07:50.220
So I think that that, this is, for me, like, the perfect amount of shmup.

00:07:50.980 --> 00:07:52.580
Is shmup sounding weird to anyone else?

00:07:53.340 --> 00:07:54.880
We're saying it enough times now.

00:07:55.040 --> 00:08:03.200
But I would say, Joe, I agree with you, but also, like, it's really cool how all of this, whether, you know, depending on which genre of the game you're playing or which part of the game you're in,

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:08.580
it's all explained very coherently in the story, you know?

00:08:09.540 --> 00:08:19.060
The idea that, like, you know, you start in the shmup form and you're kind of entering the planet and, you know, you're hitting area zero.

00:08:19.900 --> 00:08:23.240
You know, that's smart and everything like that, but that's just, like, you know, okay, that's expected.

00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:24.960
Like, that's just the start of the game or whatever.

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:34.600
But then to have, like, the lore explained to you while you're playing and then, like, the exploration aspect, like, things slowly get revealed to you.

00:08:34.700 --> 00:08:45.080
But then also where the shmup part comes in again to get you from area zero to one to two is a really neat way of, like, telling that in the story of how you're getting between the areas.

00:08:45.080 --> 00:08:50.340
And I don't think we've really seen too many games that care about mixing story and gameplay.

00:08:50.340 --> 00:08:58.740
You know, I think, not to its fault, but, like, Ninja Gaiden was one of those things where, like, story was only ever delivered in cutscenes after you finished levels.

00:08:58.900 --> 00:09:02.820
And that's fine, but it's cool to see it done while you're playing the game.

00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:13.280
Yeah, and that said, I mean, most of the story is still, like, text on a screen, which is still sort of impressive to me.

00:09:13.280 --> 00:09:21.180
Because, like, I'd say 90% of the games on this platform leave the story to exist within the contents of the manual.

00:09:21.560 --> 00:09:23.400
So I'm still happy about that.

00:09:23.560 --> 00:09:32.900
But I do also agree that I really appreciated this game's use of, like, sort of contextual or, like, interactive storytelling,

00:09:32.900 --> 00:09:39.460
where the actual things that you're doing within the game make sense to the game world,

00:09:39.460 --> 00:09:46.320
which is, like, another line that hasn't really been crossed too much in the games that we've played.

00:09:46.480 --> 00:09:47.800
Yeah, I was going to say the same thing.

00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:53.800
Like, instead of you having to, instead of it feeling like every once in a while the game tells you a little more of the story,

00:09:53.840 --> 00:09:57.820
it's like you're organically uncovering what's happening.

00:09:57.880 --> 00:10:00.120
You're discovering it for yourself as you go.

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:04.060
So it feels more like you're actually on this adventure rather than, like, your hand being held.

00:10:04.480 --> 00:10:06.160
Yeah. Environmental storytelling.

00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:07.180
That's sort of what I meant.

00:10:07.340 --> 00:10:09.040
Yeah. For sure.

00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:15.360
And I think, you know, the difference is, of course, is just most video games, you know, especially back then,

00:10:15.500 --> 00:10:18.380
were just broken up in, like, level formats, right?

00:10:18.520 --> 00:10:26.480
Like, I think Metroid and probably Zelda 2 were the only ones where it was, like, continuous gameplay from beginning to end,

00:10:26.480 --> 00:10:31.100
where, you know, like, you were just exploring the world and constantly unlocking new things.

00:10:31.280 --> 00:10:33.880
And this fits kind of nice with those games.

00:10:33.960 --> 00:10:36.800
And we're going to bring them up a lot because there's just a lot of parallels to them.

00:10:37.020 --> 00:10:41.820
I just found it surprising that I've never heard of the Guardian Legend before.

00:10:42.080 --> 00:10:46.160
Like, I'm not trying to hype the game up or anything, but when you say Legend of Zelda and Metroid,

00:10:46.400 --> 00:10:49.080
and then talk about other games in that category, you know,

00:10:49.220 --> 00:10:51.980
the fact that we're talking about the Guardian Legend, it's kind of surprising, right?

00:10:53.100 --> 00:10:59.980
Yeah. This, yeah, the fact that I still didn't know what sort of world this game took place in

00:11:00.840 --> 00:11:02.240
sort of speaks to that.

00:11:03.540 --> 00:11:10.280
And, like, it's so, it's still such an early game that you'd think that it would still be talked about,

00:11:10.820 --> 00:11:19.100
even if they thought it was bad, like, as, like, an early attempt of future exploratory games.

00:11:47.720 --> 00:11:51.960
So let's talk about that top-down exploration mode when you're the one that's going to be talked about.

00:11:51.960 --> 00:11:55.220
In the humanoid form first, because that is the bulk of the game.

00:11:55.860 --> 00:12:00.200
We describe this as Zelda-like, you know, when you're in the dungeons, you know,

00:12:00.260 --> 00:12:06.160
you have your grid and you move between squares to uncover new parts of the area.

00:12:06.280 --> 00:12:07.880
It's all nonlinear, though.

00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:14.800
And it's represented, your space in the layout of the labyrinth is, like, in X and Y.

00:12:14.800 --> 00:12:21.000
So you're in, like, X8, Y9, and you move accordingly to find new stuff.

00:12:21.440 --> 00:12:25.340
What did we think of just, like, the exploration as a whole as you're going through this?

00:12:25.460 --> 00:12:26.900
Should it have been more guided?

00:12:27.200 --> 00:12:28.880
Was it fun to explore?

00:12:29.020 --> 00:12:32.480
Was it helpful that there was a map of some sort, like a mini-map?

00:12:32.620 --> 00:12:34.760
Did that help? Joe, we'll start with you.

00:12:35.660 --> 00:12:40.100
Yeah, I think it—I really appreciate this kind of gameplay, if it's done right,

00:12:40.160 --> 00:12:44.940
where there's a lot of, like, you uncover things that you can't access yet,

00:12:44.940 --> 00:12:47.500
and you have to figure out how and then come back to it.

00:12:47.760 --> 00:12:51.200
And it—now, admittedly, I didn't beat this game.

00:12:51.320 --> 00:12:55.200
I actually, after watching some videos afterwards, like, realized this is a really long game.

00:12:55.200 --> 00:12:56.960
So I definitely have a lot more to go.

00:12:56.960 --> 00:13:03.020
But, like, I was getting to a point where I was starting to know my way around different sections of the planet, you know.

00:13:03.460 --> 00:13:05.980
And I think that was because it organically makes me remember, like,

00:13:06.040 --> 00:13:08.480
oh, okay, there's this—what do you call them?

00:13:08.500 --> 00:13:11.820
Like, a doorway or a gateway or whatever here that I don't have unlocked.

00:13:12.020 --> 00:13:13.940
Later on, I unlock moon doors.

00:13:14.020 --> 00:13:15.720
And then I'm like, oh, there was a moon door back there.

00:13:15.720 --> 00:13:16.720
And you figure that out.

00:13:16.880 --> 00:13:21.820
And I think that that was, like, a good way to get you to, like, enjoy the exploration.

00:13:22.080 --> 00:13:26.620
Because, you know, you're going through, you're fighting all these enemies on the screens and stuff,

00:13:26.620 --> 00:13:28.660
and it would be easy for it to all just kind of blur together.

00:13:28.900 --> 00:13:33.280
But it gives you reason to contextualize where you are in the world.

00:13:36.120 --> 00:13:40.540
Yeah, I like the—I like what they did with this.

00:13:40.680 --> 00:13:48.300
I think the one thing that I had a problem with was how—if it's going to be a game this big,

00:13:48.320 --> 00:13:53.880
and if it's going to be a labyrinth, I need a little bit more set dressing, I think.

00:13:53.880 --> 00:14:05.140
Like, because a game this big, a game this long, the majority of your game time is going to be sort of on—like,

00:14:05.140 --> 00:14:08.560
in these same hallways with the same, like, sort of color scheme.

00:14:08.560 --> 00:14:11.280
I don't know if it bothered you guys as much as it bothered me.

00:14:11.320 --> 00:14:17.880
And I do know that there are other, like, sort of tile sets later in the game when you get into different areas.

00:14:17.880 --> 00:14:23.540
But I did think that the environments felt a little samey.

00:14:23.840 --> 00:14:32.900
For sure. The environments definitely have that problem, you know, where if you didn't have the X and Y markings on the map,

00:14:33.080 --> 00:14:41.240
I would be very lost at times because I'd be unsure if, like, I was just heading around in a circle or what have you.

00:14:41.240 --> 00:14:49.520
But also, like, there's another problem with everything looking the samey in the sense of if you have to explore—

00:14:49.520 --> 00:14:55.420
you know, if you're the kind of gamer that I am, and you have to explore everything anyway just to make sure you don't miss any particular things.

00:14:55.420 --> 00:14:59.560
I mean, it's not like these are—it's not like there's secrets to uncover.

00:14:59.560 --> 00:15:06.680
It's just that, like, make sure you visit every place to find all the different power-ups that you could find before you move on to the next area.

00:15:07.540 --> 00:15:16.440
When you play like that, you know, it doesn't really help to make it, you know, have a lot of backtracking and have a lot of, like,

00:15:17.080 --> 00:15:19.380
oh, I'm not quite sure if I'd been here before.

00:15:19.680 --> 00:15:28.480
Like, it would have been helpful if the mini-map maybe had, like, a way that you could eventually unlock, like, where the power-ups are

00:15:28.480 --> 00:15:32.440
or if you collected that power-up so you know, like, okay, I shouldn't head back that way.

00:15:33.560 --> 00:15:36.580
It's definitely—I'm not asking for it to hold my hand or anything like that.

00:15:36.580 --> 00:15:40.200
I wouldn't want it to tell me which order and where to go for things.

00:15:41.720 --> 00:15:44.720
But, you know, for me, the exploration was missed a little

00:15:44.720 --> 00:15:49.700
because instead I was just kind of making sure that I hit every single X, Y coordinate.

00:15:50.640 --> 00:15:59.380
Yeah, and when there isn't, like, anything special about any one tile aside from, like, this is where this key is

00:15:59.380 --> 00:16:04.900
or this is where this pickup is, it's not as fun to navigate.

00:16:05.780 --> 00:16:14.580
Like, if there were some, like, cool—like, if this tile was the one with the, I don't know, the, like, ruined building in it

00:16:14.580 --> 00:16:23.440
or if there were just more—if there were just more unique landmarks, I think I could have gotten behind it a bit more.

00:16:24.180 --> 00:16:28.800
Yeah, I hear what you're saying, Natu, and I think that, like, even with the obstacles,

00:16:29.200 --> 00:16:32.680
there are always those, like, tile cover things.

00:16:33.080 --> 00:16:37.860
Like, where in Zelda you have, like, a tree and you have, you know, a rock and things like that

00:16:37.860 --> 00:16:39.320
or there's just, like, nothing like that there.

00:16:40.780 --> 00:16:47.200
Although I will say to the idea of having—making it a little more clear where the power-ups are,

00:16:48.160 --> 00:16:55.500
I mean, I'm not sure, but I imagine that the intent wasn't for people to play the way that a lot of people like us play now today

00:16:55.500 --> 00:16:57.660
where it's, like, we've got to get everything, got to unlock everything.

00:16:57.920 --> 00:17:01.480
Because I do sort of like the idea that it's, like, if we're not playing like that

00:17:01.480 --> 00:17:04.400
and we're just playing and seeing what we end up with and, like, going where we need to go

00:17:04.400 --> 00:17:06.580
and whatever power-ups we find along the way we get,

00:17:06.580 --> 00:17:10.000
it's kind of cool that, like, I can compare my character to your character

00:17:10.000 --> 00:17:11.920
and we can have, like, totally different power sets.

00:17:12.480 --> 00:17:13.820
And I think that's kind of, like, neat.

00:17:13.880 --> 00:17:16.260
It makes it a little more, like, personalized to, like, your journey.

00:17:17.120 --> 00:17:21.700
Now, the only problem there, Joe, is that the shmup stages,

00:17:21.760 --> 00:17:23.280
which we aren't going to talk about right now,

00:17:23.620 --> 00:17:26.480
you know, they're pretty darn hard, especially later on,

00:17:26.500 --> 00:17:32.460
and you can use the power-ups that you collect in the humanoid form as your shmup form.

00:17:32.460 --> 00:17:38.500
So I would argue that you want a lot of those power-ups to help you during the shmup stages.

00:17:38.740 --> 00:17:41.520
So you kind of do want to make sure you collect a lot of that stuff

00:17:41.520 --> 00:17:44.100
because if you're like me and didn't collect some of those things,

00:17:44.140 --> 00:17:47.320
you're going to have a really hard time getting through the shmup stages.

00:17:47.560 --> 00:17:50.700
Do you necessarily think that you need all of them?

00:17:50.860 --> 00:17:52.640
Like, I feel like for the shmup stages I did,

00:17:52.700 --> 00:17:55.300
I usually would find one that works really well for that stage.

00:17:55.500 --> 00:17:58.380
Yeah, you're right. But, like, I wouldn't switch between them too much.

00:17:58.380 --> 00:18:01.300
So, like, and then maybe that would also give me a reason to be like,

00:18:01.360 --> 00:18:02.440
I'm not ready for this yet.

00:18:02.440 --> 00:18:06.200
I got to go find some stuff, you know, like explore.

00:18:06.480 --> 00:18:10.920
But, yeah, then it wouldn't hurt to, I guess, have, like, a map to some of it or something like that.

00:18:12.100 --> 00:18:17.900
What you mentioned with the weapons working in both gameplay elements

00:18:18.900 --> 00:18:29.720
is what I think this game really made this something new was not only do you have this really cool animation

00:18:29.720 --> 00:18:35.540
of your space girl turning into a freaking fighter jet,

00:18:35.700 --> 00:18:45.000
but then all of those weapons that you're using on the overworld also have an equivalent in the shmup game space.

00:18:45.460 --> 00:18:51.200
I thought that that was just really neat and really, and really polishy.

00:18:51.460 --> 00:18:52.800
You know what I mean? For sure.

00:18:53.000 --> 00:18:54.940
Yeah. I thought it was a really nice touch too.

00:18:55.240 --> 00:18:58.580
You know, we didn't talk about the combat when you're the humanoid form,

00:18:58.800 --> 00:19:02.880
which thankfully is not a melee weapon like Link's sword.

00:19:02.880 --> 00:19:06.240
It's actually a gun that you shoot off.

00:19:06.240 --> 00:19:12.200
And for some reason, I couldn't tell or not, but, like, it looks like it has really good rapid fire even from the start.

00:19:12.340 --> 00:19:16.280
Like, I think it gets more rapid, but it shoots pretty constantly if you hold it down.

00:19:16.680 --> 00:19:18.020
And I appreciated that.

00:19:18.020 --> 00:19:21.880
But also just the idea that, like, when you're dealing with enemies on screen,

00:19:21.880 --> 00:19:26.740
it's a little different than how you dealt with them in Zelda because now you take on everything from a distance.

00:19:26.740 --> 00:19:29.880
You don't really want to be close to these spawning enemies.

00:19:31.660 --> 00:19:35.260
And so, you know, we were talking about, like, you know, okay, I've been here before,

00:19:35.260 --> 00:19:37.800
and it's a chore to find that out and then get out of it.

00:19:37.860 --> 00:19:44.040
But it's really not because backtracking and getting from tile to tile is actually pretty easy in this game

00:19:44.040 --> 00:19:48.920
because you can run through tiles before everything spawns,

00:19:49.520 --> 00:19:52.940
or you can shoot at things and take care of them from a distance.

00:19:52.940 --> 00:20:02.120
I appreciated the combat in this Zelda-like clone aspect of it because of the projectile versus melee weapon.

00:20:03.080 --> 00:20:10.480
Yeah. And I think they made it so that you could skip any fight, you could skip any encounter,

00:20:11.540 --> 00:20:16.280
but the economy of the game only works if you do fight some of these,

00:20:16.300 --> 00:20:18.860
and sometimes they will just straight up get in your way.

00:20:19.840 --> 00:20:27.800
So I think that they had a good balance of how much combat you could or could not actually enter.

00:20:29.380 --> 00:20:30.780
So, yeah, I agree with that.

00:20:32.040 --> 00:20:35.420
Yeah, yeah. And also, I mean, am I crazy here?

00:20:35.500 --> 00:20:37.280
Do you level up based on your score?

00:20:38.900 --> 00:20:42.880
Maybe. I leveled up and my health went up a few times randomly,

00:20:42.880 --> 00:20:44.940
and I assumed it was always like, well, I was killing things.

00:20:45.020 --> 00:20:47.560
So I just assumed it's like, oh, the more things you kill, the more you level up.

00:20:47.560 --> 00:20:53.540
So, like, that incentivized me again to attack and not just run through everything, you know?

00:20:53.680 --> 00:20:55.660
But it also never felt like I needed to go grind.

00:20:56.120 --> 00:20:59.100
Maybe it will later in the game, but so far it hasn't felt like that.

00:21:21.080 --> 00:21:23.280
Yeah, it never felt like a grind, that's for sure.

00:21:23.280 --> 00:21:30.080
And also because there's these power chips that you have to collect as well that, you know,

00:21:30.180 --> 00:21:35.780
basically that's the power chips power up your power ups, if that makes sense.

00:21:35.960 --> 00:21:41.180
But you have like 50 of these to start, and then once you run out, you can't use your,

00:21:41.320 --> 00:21:44.920
we'll call them sub weapons, so we keep saying power chips, power ups.

00:21:44.920 --> 00:21:47.900
These sub weapons can't be used once you don't have any more power chips,

00:21:48.040 --> 00:21:54.080
but you also use your power chips to buy upgrades in the shops that you find in these areas,

00:21:54.120 --> 00:21:59.960
which is a little weird that these, this like alien planet that is hurtling towards the earth

00:21:59.960 --> 00:22:05.040
also has shops on it that you can buy sub weapons to take down the said planet.

00:22:05.360 --> 00:22:06.460
But whatever.

00:22:07.220 --> 00:22:12.120
So, you know, when you do that with the power chips, it's cool that they're, they're a currency,

00:22:12.420 --> 00:22:15.820
and they also are used to fire your sub weapons.

00:22:16.540 --> 00:22:23.740
But I couldn't help feel like it was a little easy to manage the spend versus fire,

00:22:23.980 --> 00:22:29.560
because the normal combat weapon that you have is pretty darn good against most of the,

00:22:30.800 --> 00:22:33.400
the top down combat parts of the game.

00:22:33.400 --> 00:22:37.840
Even the sub bosses that you find when they like lock you into that current tile,

00:22:38.140 --> 00:22:41.100
you know, like an alarm goes off, and then all of a sudden you're locked in,

00:22:41.160 --> 00:22:41.980
and you have to fight the sub boss.

00:22:42.220 --> 00:22:46.500
Even that sub boss can be taken down with just your usual weapon,

00:22:46.500 --> 00:22:49.080
so you don't have to use sub weapons that often.

00:22:49.080 --> 00:22:52.520
And when you do, it either doesn't cost a lot of power chips,

00:22:52.580 --> 00:22:56.700
or you can probably find power chips in a nearby tile pretty easily.

00:22:56.700 --> 00:23:00.240
I never found, I never felt like I was running out of this cache.

00:23:01.700 --> 00:23:05.700
Yeah, well, I actually felt like, um, I used the sub weapons quite a bit,

00:23:05.920 --> 00:23:07.780
like probably most of the time.

00:23:08.280 --> 00:23:11.180
Or I was like using both, hitting both A and B at the same time.

00:23:11.480 --> 00:23:14.540
Not because I felt like my last, what's that?

00:23:14.760 --> 00:23:16.380
You just like using that lightsaber.

00:23:17.860 --> 00:23:19.220
The two-sided one?

00:23:20.540 --> 00:23:23.140
There's a, yeah, I think, well, first it's just,

00:23:23.340 --> 00:23:25.360
first it's just like a beam sword, right?

00:23:25.360 --> 00:23:27.100
Like it's just, yeah.

00:23:27.820 --> 00:23:29.820
Yeah, and then I don't even think I had that one.

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:32.480
I had like a double-sided beam sword.

00:23:32.680 --> 00:23:34.020
I think that's the upgrade, yeah.

00:23:34.400 --> 00:23:36.940
Oh, okay, I must have not even used it while it was normal

00:23:36.940 --> 00:23:38.740
and upgraded it or something.

00:23:38.960 --> 00:23:41.240
The reason that I was using these sub weapons a lot

00:23:41.240 --> 00:23:43.800
was not necessarily because I thought my blaster wasn't good enough,

00:23:43.820 --> 00:23:47.060
but because it helped me to not die a lot.

00:23:47.200 --> 00:23:49.960
It helped me kill things quick enough that they wouldn't hit me first.

00:23:50.360 --> 00:23:52.260
So like it was fun to kind of see like,

00:23:52.320 --> 00:23:53.700
oh, okay, there's a crowd of people.

00:23:53.820 --> 00:23:57.300
I'm going to use the, there's like the one that circles around you

00:23:57.300 --> 00:23:59.380
and that'll take out, it's like good for crowd control.

00:23:59.580 --> 00:24:02.300
And then there's other than just more powerful versions of your blaster.

00:24:02.460 --> 00:24:04.140
And there's one that'll just go in a straight line

00:24:04.140 --> 00:24:05.980
and kill everything on that path.

00:24:06.100 --> 00:24:09.040
And like there's a lot of reasons to switch up.

00:24:09.140 --> 00:24:12.160
So like I ended up using them actually most of the time.

00:24:12.760 --> 00:24:17.020
Yeah, I did appreciate that they actually seem to have like a utility.

00:24:17.020 --> 00:24:20.120
They're not just like for fun.

00:24:20.320 --> 00:24:22.760
Like there's been a lot of games that they have power-ups,

00:24:22.920 --> 00:24:27.580
but there's either like one that's obviously the most efficient

00:24:27.580 --> 00:24:30.040
and there's no reason to use the others.

00:24:31.320 --> 00:24:34.380
So yeah, I like that these sort of had different use cases.

00:24:34.640 --> 00:24:40.500
I think the one that I found to be the most useful in like the overworld bosses,

00:24:41.860 --> 00:24:45.500
or at least the few that I actually played,

00:24:46.820 --> 00:24:52.080
was one that was sort of like it send out waves behind you at 45 degree angles.

00:24:52.320 --> 00:24:54.220
Like the icon is like a Y.

00:24:54.980 --> 00:25:01.080
So that made it much easier for you to actually like move more evasively

00:25:01.080 --> 00:25:03.460
while still landing some blows on the boss.

00:25:03.460 --> 00:25:10.480
So I think that was a good example of like these weapons aren't just bigger blaster.

00:25:11.240 --> 00:25:16.660
Yeah, yeah. Way more thought put into them than I think we're used to so far for the most part.

00:25:16.800 --> 00:25:19.360
And there's 12 of them, which is a large amount.

00:25:19.500 --> 00:25:21.260
But then as Sean mentioned, they upgrade.

00:25:21.320 --> 00:25:26.200
So there's actually, I think, technically 36 then by that,

00:25:26.280 --> 00:25:28.620
because I think that you can each upgrade three times or something like that.

00:25:28.620 --> 00:25:34.620
Don't quote me on exactly 36, but like that's a ton of variations in, you know,

00:25:35.080 --> 00:25:39.020
and forget about just like a Zelda-like game, but in a shmup.

00:25:39.240 --> 00:25:41.880
Usually in a shmup, I think the most we ever saw was like 12,

00:25:41.900 --> 00:25:44.580
and we were like kind of amazed by that, right?

00:25:44.740 --> 00:25:46.600
Like I forget which game had that.

00:25:46.780 --> 00:25:48.520
Maybe it was like Zanuck or something.

00:25:48.740 --> 00:25:52.320
But that's pretty cool that like there's so many combinations here.

00:25:52.400 --> 00:25:55.620
And as Joe mentioned, you can compare builds with other people

00:25:55.620 --> 00:25:59.720
because, you know, he was talking about how he went right to the double-bladed sword.

00:25:59.960 --> 00:26:04.700
But, you know, some people like, you know, need certain kind of weapons earlier,

00:26:04.700 --> 00:26:09.740
but you don't need any of the weapons to actually like progress, right?

00:26:09.880 --> 00:26:14.260
Like you could theoretically just collect the necessary amount of power chips

00:26:14.260 --> 00:26:19.320
for the upgrades to get the keys that would let you go to the next,

00:26:19.320 --> 00:26:22.260
the shmup levels are described as corridors,

00:26:22.320 --> 00:26:26.160
and the Zelda-like stages are described as areas.

00:26:26.440 --> 00:26:28.540
So you could just keep going between area and corridor,

00:26:28.540 --> 00:26:31.140
just doing the necessary stuff without using any of it.

00:26:31.320 --> 00:26:33.460
Yeah, it all comes down to like personal play style.

00:26:34.120 --> 00:26:37.820
And your regular gun actually also upgrades too.

00:26:38.020 --> 00:26:44.460
And I think when you start to use a sub-weapon, it actually downgrades.

00:26:45.440 --> 00:26:47.300
So that's also sort of adding to the balance.

00:26:47.520 --> 00:26:53.680
But, yeah, even your regular pea shooter can turn into a giant like wave gun sort of thing.

00:26:54.280 --> 00:26:57.620
Yeah, the whole thing gives me like, it's going to sound funny,

00:26:57.680 --> 00:26:59.620
but it gives me like real Ratchet and Clank vibes.

00:26:59.620 --> 00:27:02.500
It's just like find the next cool weapon, upgrade it,

00:27:02.580 --> 00:27:05.240
and you get your whole arsenal, you know, figured out.

00:27:05.380 --> 00:27:06.960
You're exploring this world.

00:27:07.820 --> 00:27:09.060
There's flying levels.

00:27:09.200 --> 00:27:10.460
I mean, I don't know why.

00:27:10.560 --> 00:27:11.920
It's just giving me Ratchet and Clank vibes.

00:27:11.920 --> 00:27:15.940
And Ratchet and Clank is one of your best ofs, right?

00:27:17.160 --> 00:27:19.260
I'm a big Ratchet and Clank guy, yes.

00:27:20.900 --> 00:27:22.340
The Ratchet and Clank caucus.

00:27:23.740 --> 00:27:28.520
In the Zelda games, you get those old men who give you –

00:27:28.520 --> 00:27:29.480
sorry, Zelda 1.

00:27:29.760 --> 00:27:35.020
In Zelda 2, there's some wise people though in the town that give you clues as well.

00:27:35.160 --> 00:27:36.800
But those old men would give you –

00:27:36.800 --> 00:27:38.160
Dirty old men give you –

00:27:38.160 --> 00:27:44.400
Yeah, they would give you the clues and those would be like hints that were very vague

00:27:45.120 --> 00:27:47.140
or were just very straightforward.

00:27:47.500 --> 00:27:51.200
In this game, you get hints in a similar way where the –

00:27:51.200 --> 00:27:56.500
either the shopkeepers or places that you find have these story beats

00:27:56.500 --> 00:28:00.360
or they have how to get to the next corridor.

00:28:01.720 --> 00:28:03.100
What were we thinking about these?

00:28:03.280 --> 00:28:07.780
Were they straightforward enough or did it require some actual like,

00:28:08.160 --> 00:28:09.620
huh, I wonder what they meant by that?

00:28:09.720 --> 00:28:11.280
I think a little bit of both.

00:28:11.500 --> 00:28:14.740
I mean, there were some times where I was like,

00:28:14.780 --> 00:28:15.900
oh, that's perfectly straightforward.

00:28:16.080 --> 00:28:17.100
I think somewhere towards the beginning,

00:28:17.240 --> 00:28:18.460
there's something that says like,

00:28:18.640 --> 00:28:21.600
you'll need a special weapon to get through the barrier or something like that

00:28:21.600 --> 00:28:27.440
to get through the wall. And I was playing for a little bit and I got to the next area and there was a dead end.

00:28:27.780 --> 00:28:30.220
And then I kept playing and I got back to that first thing and I was like,

00:28:30.380 --> 00:28:32.600
oh, I'm supposed to use the grenades to get through that.

00:28:32.780 --> 00:28:35.460
Went all the way back through, used the grenades and just didn't do anything.

00:28:35.740 --> 00:28:37.940
And I was like, okay, maybe it wasn't as obvious as I thought.

00:28:38.060 --> 00:28:39.620
But it was a little bit of a head scratcher.

00:28:39.680 --> 00:28:41.360
I was like, okay, what is this asking me to do?

00:28:42.060 --> 00:28:43.600
But it was nice to –

00:28:43.600 --> 00:28:45.120
I think it was like a good balance.

00:28:45.500 --> 00:28:46.800
What did you end up having to do?

00:28:46.800 --> 00:28:48.940
I don't know what that was telling me to do, actually.

00:28:49.880 --> 00:28:54.460
I moved on and I didn't end up figuring out what barrier they were talking about.

00:28:54.500 --> 00:28:56.200
So I haven't done it yet.

00:28:56.640 --> 00:29:00.580
All right. But at least the game wasn't talking to you in like riddles, right?

00:29:00.740 --> 00:29:05.540
Like it wasn't anything so cryptic or a terrible translation

00:29:05.540 --> 00:29:07.960
that you couldn't figure out what to do next.

00:29:08.020 --> 00:29:09.840
But, you know, we also have the luxury of guides.

00:29:10.040 --> 00:29:13.240
And I totally went on Strategy Wiki for portions of this

00:29:13.240 --> 00:29:15.720
just to see where certain power-ups were located and stuff.

00:29:15.720 --> 00:29:16.760
And there's no shame in that.

00:29:17.080 --> 00:29:21.000
Yeah. And, you know, the game itself used –

00:29:22.880 --> 00:29:26.880
it had a good use of the characters it was allowed

00:29:28.360 --> 00:29:30.640
where it was able to still make complete sentences,

00:29:30.640 --> 00:29:32.580
which I think is a big problem we have in other things.

00:29:32.640 --> 00:29:33.880
It's like, oh, there's only five characters?

00:29:33.880 --> 00:29:35.800
We can only make three words here?

00:29:35.920 --> 00:29:37.500
They'll get it. They'll know what we're talking about.

00:29:37.640 --> 00:29:39.320
Nobody has any clue what it's trying to say.

00:29:39.600 --> 00:29:41.480
So this one was refreshing.

00:29:41.760 --> 00:29:44.000
It's much better than Simon's Quest in that regard.

00:29:44.980 --> 00:29:45.820
Right, right.

00:29:45.980 --> 00:29:50.400
And the overall objective of these humanoid stages

00:29:50.400 --> 00:29:55.980
is that you have to make sure that you deactivate these 10 safety devices –

00:29:55.980 --> 00:29:58.280
or, sorry, activate. You don't want to deactivate them.

00:29:58.320 --> 00:30:03.280
You want to activate the 10 safety devices that are in each of the areas.

00:30:04.000 --> 00:30:09.000
And, you know, doing that will stop the planet on its course to Earth.

00:30:09.300 --> 00:30:10.440
Well, it's a self-destruct.

00:30:10.440 --> 00:30:13.700
Yeah, I think you are deactivating the safety devices

00:30:14.560 --> 00:30:16.700
so you can self-destruct the planet.

00:30:16.900 --> 00:30:18.640
It's actually very unsafe.

00:30:19.620 --> 00:30:20.440
Yeah, yeah.

00:30:20.520 --> 00:30:24.020
But doesn't the game word it like you have to activate safety devices?

00:30:24.380 --> 00:30:29.240
So do they mean that they're safety devices for the player?

00:30:29.640 --> 00:30:32.500
I guess safety is a relative term, you know?

00:30:32.840 --> 00:30:33.880
Yeah, yeah.

00:30:33.880 --> 00:30:37.820
I thought it was like the evil planet, which I don't think we've described yet.

00:30:37.940 --> 00:30:41.220
Like, you're on a planet that is traveling towards Earth.

00:30:41.500 --> 00:30:44.840
Like, an evil planet is traveling towards Earth to destroy Earth.

00:30:45.160 --> 00:30:48.580
It wasn't always evil, but over time it just got more evil.

00:30:48.720 --> 00:30:49.760
No, it's become evil, yes.

00:30:50.220 --> 00:30:54.400
And that planet has safety devices to prevent it from self-destructing.

00:30:54.480 --> 00:30:57.060
And you need to deactivate it so you can self-destruct it.

00:30:57.080 --> 00:30:58.900
I believe. Could be wrong.

00:30:59.800 --> 00:31:01.320
Okay, that makes more sense to me.

00:31:01.440 --> 00:31:02.580
If you're going to get into the plot, though,

00:31:02.640 --> 00:31:06.240
we might as well talk about that shocking reveal when you first become humanoid

00:31:06.240 --> 00:31:10.440
and it has the person who previously tried to do all this

00:31:10.440 --> 00:31:15.480
and it was like, I failed, but here's what you have to do.

00:31:15.640 --> 00:31:17.260
You know, like, if you're reading this, I'm dead,

00:31:17.400 --> 00:31:19.100
but here's what you need to do.

00:31:19.280 --> 00:31:24.340
And I like to think that that is, like, previous versions of the Guardian

00:31:24.720 --> 00:31:25.960
that tried to pull this off.

00:31:25.960 --> 00:31:30.560
Like, your other, when you get game overs, that's like, you go back and that's you.

00:31:30.800 --> 00:31:32.900
You're just a clone. Yeah, exactly.

00:31:33.120 --> 00:31:37.620
Like, now, you know, like the next, you know, similar to, like, Nier Automata

00:31:37.620 --> 00:31:40.420
where, like, you know, the next version of 2B.

00:31:40.880 --> 00:31:43.140
Yeah, which, no, I don't think that's a huge spoiler.

00:31:43.440 --> 00:31:47.380
I'm spoiled. But overall, you know, the plot, as wacky as that sounds,

00:31:47.600 --> 00:31:48.760
I was into it, you know?

00:31:48.900 --> 00:31:51.480
Giant planet hurtling towards another planet.

00:31:51.740 --> 00:31:52.760
That sounds intense.

00:31:53.620 --> 00:31:56.480
Yeah, I liked the format of, like, the lost log.

00:31:58.200 --> 00:32:01.620
Like, I'm, yeah, the whole I'm dead, but you can do this.

00:32:01.760 --> 00:32:06.660
I dug that in terms of, like, a form of storytelling.

00:32:07.040 --> 00:32:07.980
Yeah, oh, me too.

00:32:10.120 --> 00:32:14.820
But let's get back to those self-detonation devices for a second

00:32:14.820 --> 00:32:17.300
because that's, you know, if that's your objective, right,

00:32:17.340 --> 00:32:22.080
and that's what you want to make sure you do, you know, to do that

00:32:22.080 --> 00:32:25.480
and just to explore in general, one part that we haven't really talked about

00:32:25.480 --> 00:32:28.600
is that other than enemies and tiles,

00:32:29.200 --> 00:32:33.640
inside of each of those, you know, like, tile areas that you go to,

00:32:33.640 --> 00:32:38.220
there are these, like, gray blocks that you have to shoot at,

00:32:38.240 --> 00:32:41.340
and a lot of them are just nothing at all.

00:32:41.460 --> 00:32:43.320
But occasionally, like, you know, you'll shoot at one

00:32:43.320 --> 00:32:49.420
and it'll either reveal a power-up or reveal a hole to another part of the area

00:32:49.420 --> 00:32:53.780
or reveal something else, you know?

00:32:53.840 --> 00:32:56.840
Like, it's just, it's planted by the game.

00:32:56.980 --> 00:32:59.080
Like, it's designed to be in those specific spots.

00:32:59.080 --> 00:33:00.520
It's not random or anything.

00:33:00.520 --> 00:33:04.260
Even the power chips, they're perfectly placed where they need to be.

